      "created_at": "2017-10-04T18:05:49Z",
      "updated_at": "2017-10-04T18:05:49Z",
      "body": "For this to make sense (be applicable as a legally valid means of tracking acceptance of the ToS), it will need to do more than simply track the fact that somebody clicked the checkbox. \r\n\r\nNot a lawyer, but from my corporate days, I recall it also needs to ask for positive confirmation of receipt of the ToS.\r\n\r\nA checkbox isn't enough, it has to be \"signed\" and agreed to, using one of the legally accepted electronic signature options and in the context of the ToS text. Thus it can't use the supplied user email from the email field as a \"signature\". Also can't use IP address either since the IP address is by no means not unique to the user who agreed. \r\n\r\nThen it has to be stored somewhere with a clear audit trail associated with it. \r\n\r\nAdding a timestamp and/or IP address is not going to cut it in a dispute. Not even close, unfortunately.",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "pin": null,
